01:06:27,510 --> 01:06:30,370
And the reason for that is because there's a double here,

657
01:06:31,030 --> 01:06:34,490
and it should reside on a boundary

658
01:06:34,490 --> 01:06:37,230
that its starting address should be a multiple of eight.

659
01:06:38,080 --> 01:06:40,160
And you'll see as long as this is eight,

660
01:06:40,180 --> 01:06:45,560
a multiple of eight, and this offset here is a multiple of eight, then this address

661
01:06:45,560 --> 01:06:46,700
will be a multiple of eight.

662
01:06:47,530 --> 01:06:49,350
So that's the idea of alignment.

663
01:06:51,790 --> 01:06:53,570
And so why is that true?

664
01:06:53,670 --> 01:07:01,380
Well, it's really a hardware issue, that the memory system is sort of, the actual hardware memory memory

665
01:07:01,380 --> 01:07:03,960
memory does not reference one byte at a time.

666
01:07:04,040 --> 01:07:10,100
It references, actually, in most machines nowadays, about 64 bytes at a time,

667
01:07:10,700 --> 01:07:11,580
depending on.

668
01:07:11,820 --> 01:07:14,720
various widths within the hardware.

669
01:07:14,720 --> 01:07:19,140
And in general, if a particular piece of data crosses

670
01:07:19,140 --> 01:07:26,540
the boundaries between two blocks that are because of a misaligned address, that will take extra

671
01:07:26,540 --> 01:07:31,060
steps by the hardware and potentially even the operating system to deal with.

672
01:07:31,060 --> 01:07:36,500
So just for efficiency reason, they say, do this alignment, trust me.

673
01:07:36,500 --> 01:07:41,800
In x86 machines, if you have unaligned data, it will execute just fine.

674
01:07:41,820 --> 01:07:43,740
it just might run a little bit more slowly.

675
01:07:44,280 --> 01:07:47,760
On some other machines, if you try to do an unaligned access,

676
01:07:47,880 --> 01:07:50,600
it will actually cause a memory fault.

677
01:07:51,420 --> 01:07:51,880
Question.

678
01:07:52,380 --> 01:07:55,260
If by default generates aligned data?

679
01:07:55,820 --> 01:07:56,120
Yes.

680
01:08:02,480 --> 01:08:05,700
The question was, how could you force it to generate unaligned data?

681
01:08:06,600 --> 01:08:08,740
I don't know if you could.

682
01:08:08,740 --> 01:08:16,620
And we'll talk about ways you can sort of be more clever in how you do your allocation

683
01:08:16,620 --> 01:08:19,040
to avoid wasting space.

684
01:08:21,240 --> 01:08:28,900
So in general then, we have, we're mostly looking here at data types that are either one, two, or four,

685
01:08:29,060 --> 01:08:30,000
eight bytes long.

686
01:08:30,600 --> 01:08:35,820
There are some 16-byte data types, but we won't be looking at those.

687
01:08:36,400 --> 01:08:42,360
And in general, with the address, then, you can tell the alignment of an address by how many zeros,

688
01:08:43,000 --> 01:08:47,940
if it's a power of two alignment, how many zeros are at the end of a bit-level representation?

689
01:08:47,960 --> 01:08:48,760
of that address.

690
01:08:50,850 --> 01:08:53,490
And so as we showed, in general, the C compiler,

691
01:08:53,690 --> 01:08:58,010
when it's creating the layout for a structure, a struct,

692
01:08:58,010 --> 01:09:03,700
a struct, will put in padding bytes in there to make the alignment work.

693
01:09:05,770 --> 01:09:11,770
The other thing it will do is, if it need be, it will add bytes to the end to make

694
01:09:11,770 --> 01:09:16,690
sure that the overall size of the data structure meets whatever underlying alignment

695
01:09:16,690 --> 01:09:17,810
requirement there is.

696
01:09:17,810 --> 01:09:19,570
And this one, because it contains a dot.

697
01:09:19,590 --> 01:09:26,940
the overall data structure has to be aligned on an 8 byte boundary.

698
01:09:26,940 --> 01:09:36,730
And the reasoning for that is, imagine I had an array of structs, of these structs in particular,

699
01:09:36,730 --> 01:09:44,610
then if I can assume that, if I can ensure that this array address is a multiple of 8,

700
01:09:44,610 --> 01:09:50,730
then you can see that each successive element of this array, each of these structs is a multiple

701
01:09:50,730 --> 01:09:56,430
of 8. And then within that struct, I've ensured that each field that requires an alignment

702
01:09:56,430 --> 01:10:04,930
by 8 is positioned with an offset that's a multiple of 8, and so it all works out. But if you tried to tinker

703
01:10:04,930 --> 01:10:21,150
with this by making this too short, then you start getting misaligned references. And so one thing

704
01:10:21,150 --> 01:10:26,990
you'll see is for every struct, the actual alignment requirement, depends on what's the sort of

705
01:10:26,990 --> 01:10:32,230
worst case alignment, the maximum alignment of any element. So this particular

706
01:10:32,230 --> 01:10:40,430
structure, for example, contains a float V, which has a size of 4. And so this would

707
01:10:40,430 --> 01:10:50,090
be an alignment of 4. And you'll see in the code, in general, when it has to, this,

708
01:10:50,090 --> 01:10:57,190
all this address arithmetic we've seen gets used in various different ways to scale values, compute

709
01:10:57,190 --> 01:11:02,110
offsets and so forth to get the appropriate elements.

710
01:11:02,110 --> 01:11:07,810
So your question before is, could I tell the compiler not to do alignment?

711
01:11:07,810 --> 01:11:17,160
And the better way is to declare your fields in a way that minimizes the amount of wasted space.

712
01:11:17,160 --> 01:11:25,560
So, for example, here, I wasted 3 bytes here and 3 bytes here to meet the alignment requirements.

713
01:11:25,560 --> 01:11:28,740
But if I just put I at the beginning and then C and D,

714
01:11:28,760 --> 01:11:48,940
I would have only wasted 2 bytes total. And in general, because all the alignment requirements are powers of 2, the sort of greedy algorithm works. It says, if I just take, put the biggest stuff at the beginning and then successfully smaller elements, I will be able to minimize any wasted space.

715
01:11:48,940 --> 01:11:59,500
And that's a general property. But this is a case where you as the writer of C code have that effect.

716
01:11:59,500 --> 01:12:02,360
The compiler will not do this for you automatically.

717
01:12:02,600 --> 01:12:02,900
Question.

718
01:12:02,900 --> 01:12:07,950
Why is that a part of the case of having an array?

719
01:12:08,590 --> 01:12:09,030
What's that?

720
01:12:10,130 --> 01:12:13,270
Oh, but remember, an array, I don't care about the array.

721
01:12:13,390 --> 01:12:15,190
I care about the array. I care about the elements of the array.

722
01:12:15,770 --> 01:12:22,590
Because this is only down at the lowest level where I'm accessing either a single int, a single double, or a pointer,

723
01:12:23,390 --> 01:12:25,550
and not all the rest of the stuff, remember.

724
01:12:25,610 --> 01:12:28,330
I never read an entire array from memory.

725
01:12:28,830 --> 01:12:30,650
So alignment only.

726
01:12:30,810 --> 01:12:38,400
It refers down to the primitive types, not the aggregate types.

727
01:12:38,400 --> 01:12:41,400
If this is confusing, the book covers this fairly carefully.

728
01:12:41,400 --> 01:12:47,160
So let me just finish up with a little glimpse of floating point.

729
01:12:47,160 --> 01:12:53,340
Floating point is a complicated history in x86.

730
01:12:53,340 --> 01:13:02,540
Back in the really ancient days, there was a chip that went with the 8086 processor called the 8087.

731
01:13:02,540 --> 01:13:05,540
And at the time it was a masterpiece of engineering.

732
01:13:05,540 --> 01:13:11,300
piece of engineering to be able to put all the hardware necessary to implement full

733
01:13:11,300 --> 01:13:13,820
I-Triple-E floating point on a single chip.

734
01:13:13,820 --> 01:13:22,100
And it was the first one of its type. In fact, it was sort of co-developed with the I-Triple-E

735
01:13:22,100 --> 01:13:28,310
floating point standard itself. But the programming model for it is horrendously awful and

736
01:13:28,310 --> 01:13:34,110
ugly. I used to, in older editions of the book, there were, it was in the first

737
01:13:34,110 --> 01:13:38,350
edition. It was in a web aside in the second edition. And now it's

738
01:13:38,350 --> 01:13:48,060
been expunged completely because it's so awful. But more recently, in the world of x86, they realized

739
01:13:48,060 --> 01:13:54,200
that to support things like video and all the stuff people actually do with their machines,

740
01:13:54,200 --> 01:14:01,660
they needed some more horsepower in numeric processing. And they implemented a class of

741
01:14:01,660 --> 01:14:12,320
instructions called SSE, which stands for SIMD, something SIMD

742
01:14:12,320 --> 01:14:18,800
execution. Anyways, and I'll show you in a minute what that means. And that's now, in the more

743
01:14:18,800 --> 01:14:25,850
recent versions of x86, this has been juiced up to something called AVX, which,

744
01:14:25,850 --> 01:14:31,890
but the shark machines support this, a version of what's called SSE. So let me just

745
01:14:31,890 --> 01:14:35,850
give you a feeling for that. And in particular, the shark machine support

746
01:14:35,850 --> 01:14:42,750
what's called SSE version 3. And what it is is that there's 16 special registers, distinct

747
01:14:42,750 --> 01:14:48,490
from the other registers we've talked about before, each of which has 16 bytes. And

748
01:14:48,490 --> 01:14:53,310
then there's operations that can operate on those and treat them in different ways. So

749
01:14:53,310 --> 01:15:00,680
So one is to treat this such a register as an array as an array of 16 cares, or as eight

750
01:15:00,680 --> 01:15:11,600
shorts or four ints or and also to support a double floating point arithmetic.

751
01:15:11,600 --> 01:15:17,960
So this stuff was sort of going on and somebody had the observation that said, well, I could just

752
01:15:17,960 --> 01:15:23,780
then sort of subset all this and implement the floating point operations directly using

753
01:15:23,780 --> 01:15:30,810
this SSE stuff and skip the old x-87 stuff. And so that's the way floating point

754
01:15:30,810 --> 01:15:35,310
voting point is now generated on, say, the shark machines and most recent machines.

755
01:15:36,620 --> 01:15:43,460
This is a, this is again an example of where the world is more complicated than it should have to be.

756
01:15:44,000 --> 01:15:51,440
So, for example, there is an instruction that says, add SS, so add a single scalar, is what that

757
01:15:51,440 --> 01:16:00,560
notation, add a single precision scalar, add a scalar, add a scalar single precision scalar, add a scalar,

758
01:16:01,140 --> 01:16:04,180
and it looks a little like. And it looks a little like the, uh, and it looks a scalar

759
01:16:04,180 --> 01:16:11,180
instructions, the ad Q instructions you've seen, that it increments, it adds the source to the

760
01:16:11,180 --> 01:16:20,030
destination. There's ways I can do four ads in a, all at the same time, using this is what they call

761
01:16:20,030 --> 01:16:34,470
CIMD execution, which stands for a single instruction, multiple data. One instruction, the add PS, P stands for P stands for pact, we'll do, we'll do, we'll perform

762
01:16:34,630 --> 01:16:37,970
that one instruction will do add with four different numbers.

763
01:16:39,580 --> 01:16:43,540
And similarly, you can do it with double precision.

764
01:16:43,740 --> 01:16:45,640
You can do one of them, you can do a PAP.

765
01:16:46,760 --> 01:16:51,320
And so actually, the underlying, and we'll see this when we'll look at code optimization,

766
01:16:52,220 --> 01:16:57,520
if you write code to make use of these instructions, you can really boost the performance of the computer.

767
01:16:58,850 --> 01:17:02,790
But this just gives you a flavor for what floating point code looks like.

768
01:17:03,070 --> 01:17:05,630
And the rules are pretty simple, that just as we saw before,

769
01:17:05,810 --> 01:17:09,810
that arguments get passed in registers.

770
01:17:09,810 --> 01:17:14,810
Well, floating point arguments get passed in these xMMM registers.

771
01:17:14,810 --> 01:17:15,810
XMMM.

772
01:17:15,810 --> 01:17:19,810
You don't have to memorize RDI, RSI, RSI, RDS.

773
01:17:19,810 --> 01:17:22,810
They're in XMMMMM, 0, 2.

774
01:17:22,810 --> 01:17:23,810
It's all very logical.

775
01:17:23,810 --> 01:17:28,810
And the return value is in XMMMMMM0, and all the registers are caller save.

776
01:17:28,810 --> 01:17:30,810
There's no call-y-save.

777
01:17:30,810 --> 01:17:33,810
So something like adding two floating point numbers

778
01:17:33,810 --> 01:17:35,810
will just look like,

779
01:17:35,810 --> 01:17:40,970
a single floating point addition instruction.

780
01:17:40,970 --> 01:17:43,610
Same with double.

781
01:17:43,610 --> 01:17:49,770
And if you mix together code that has a pointer and a floating point operator,

782
01:17:49,770 --> 01:17:53,770
the pointer will be passed in a regular register, RDI,

783
01:17:53,770 --> 01:17:56,770
and this double will be passed in XMMMM0.

784
01:17:56,770 --> 01:18:00,770
So it sort of, as it goes through the argument list,

785
01:18:00,770 --> 01:18:05,770
you sort of, in the particular order, if it's an integer or a pointer,

786
01:18:05,770 --> 01:18:08,770
it's in one of the R registers.

787
01:18:08,770 --> 01:18:10,770
And if it's a floating point value,

788
01:18:10,770 --> 01:18:13,770
it's in one of the XMM registers,

789
01:18:13,770 --> 01:18:18,200
and potentially interleaved.

790
01:18:18,200 --> 01:18:20,460
Almost done.

791
01:18:20,460 --> 01:18:24,460
And so this is a code that does this particular operation,

792
01:18:24,460 --> 01:18:27,460
and it's interesting because it's returning a,

793
01:18:27,460 --> 01:18:29,460
it's doing memory referencing,

794
01:18:29,460 --> 01:18:31,460
so again, there's instructions

795
01:18:31,460 --> 01:18:35,460
that will read from memory and copy into an XM register.

796
01:18:35,460 --> 01:18:37,750
You can add registers.

797
01:18:37,750 --> 01:18:44,750
you can make a copy from one XM register to another, and you can store results.

798
01:18:44,750 --> 01:18:51,750
So this code looks a lot like what you do in integer arithmetic, except it's using XMMM

799
01:18:51,750 --> 01:18:56,750
and special floating point instructions rather than the ones you've seen before.

800
01:18:56,750 --> 01:18:59,750
So the basic idea of it is fairly simple.

801
01:18:59,750 --> 01:19:04,750
It gets way more complicated because there are lots of instructions.

802
01:19:04,750 --> 01:19:06,750
Double, float, on and up.

803
01:19:06,750 --> 01:19:11,880
On and on and on, there's a special instruction to do square root and all this stuff.

804
01:19:11,880 --> 01:19:16,880
And the comparison stuff is really nasty and messy and hard to understand.

805
01:19:16,880 --> 01:19:21,910
And then you'll see various tricks to use constant values too.

806
01:19:21,910 --> 01:19:23,910
So it's covered in the book.

807
01:19:23,910 --> 01:19:26,910
We're not going to really talk about it much in this course.

808
01:19:26,910 --> 01:19:30,910
But the underlying, the basic principles of it are fairly straightforward.

809
01:19:30,910 --> 01:19:33,040
Okay, that'll do us for today.

